            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="false" portableAirConditioning="true" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="1" energyStarUnits="1" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 151 litre chimney vented gas hot water tank. With the air tightness of your home, this appliance is having troubles receiving enough air for combustion &amp; proper venting up the chimney - this can be seen with the melted plastic grommets at the top of the tank, where combustion gases are spilling out under the rim of the chimney vent collar. I would suggest that you upgrade to a direct vent hot water heater &amp; I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 99 per year in energy costs, , decrease your annual energy usage by 2.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older (1999) condensing gas furnace (originally rated at 92% AFUE). As this furnace is now 20 years old, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. The high efficiency motor will also help save electricity while you use that furnace fan to distribute wood stove heat &amp; ventilation air from the HRV.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, could potentially save $ 152 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Wood Stove Combustion Air &amp; Circulation



You have a newer Blaze King Princess wood stove that operates at 81% efficiency. It has the opportunity to have an outside combustion air supply directly connected to the back of the stove, however this is not currently installed. Based on how air tight your house is and the evidence of back-drafting combustion fumes back into the house - I suggest you have the dedicated combustion air supply pipe installed before the 2019/20 heating season starts.



For circulation of the wood stove heat throughout the house, I suggest you use the furnace circulation fan to help accomplish this. Having a return air supply from the upper basement wall area in the basement would work well. I saw 2 houses in Hay River in July where they had a separate thermostat in the basement wired into their furnace that was programmed to turn on the furnace fan only whenever the basement temperature (from the wood stove) reached a programmed high temperature. When you are shopping for a new furnace, you should check with your heating contractor to determine if the furnace has the capability of:

1. Turning on (in heat supply mode) when the main floor thermostat calls for heat

2. Turning on (in circ fan mode) when the HRV is on

3. Turning on (in circ fan mode) when a basement thermostat reaches an upper level temp setpoint



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a Heat Recovery Ventilation (Flex 100H-ES)       

        - a range hood fan (that is currently not venting out - we located the vent shield)

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



There is also a fresh air supply duct connected to furnace ductwork to bring in some outside fresh air each time the furnace operates and whenever one of the above exhaust fans is operating. There is a manual damper on this fresh air (located behind the furnace where the pipe connects to the furnace ductwork - so not very convenient) that you can adjust to determine how much fresh air enters the house throughout the year. At the time of my evaluation, the fresh air damper was open. The "opening" on the exterior of the house for this fresh air supply is right at the surface level of the deck &amp; there is no screen - I would expect this creates an entry point (at least into the furnace ductwork)  for mice &amp; bugs, and I suspect the opening becomes blocked with snow in winter.



Your house is generally air tight and it would require an HRV if it was being constructed today. You recently had the HRV installed, however when a house is mostly finished inside (including the basement) there are not many reasonable options to connect the HRV supply &amp; return air ductwork. So ........ the easiest &amp; least intrusive for your house was to have the supply and exhaust pipes from the HRV connect directly to the return &amp; supply air ductwork of the furnace. It would be better to have the exhaust air to the HRV come from the bathroom &amp; main living areas (rather than just from the supply plenum of the furnace), however very difficult to retrofit this into your house. Having the supply air from the HRV go to the return air plenum is OK.



For the HRV to truly function well, it required an electrical interconnection to the furnace fan so the furnace fan will come on &amp; better distribute the HRV air supply throughout the house. When the furnace fan is not on, the HRV air ventilation is mostly just "short circuiting" through the furnace itself &amp; doing very little for your house. The fact that you use your woodstove for the majority of heating your house also reduced the furnace &amp; HRV run-time. It would be good to use your furnace fan to evenly distribute the woodstove heat throughout the house.



For the main floor bathroom, you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 11.2 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current gas hot water tank and the wood stove vent up a chimney and are both susceptible to back drafting. In fact - we could see evidence of back-drafting on the top of the hot water tank.



During my assessment of your home, I did see one carbon monoxide (CO) detector (in a basement bedroom - however this location does not well serve the rest of the house) and no smoke detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climah transportation costs, and climate change!

